  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My enumeration (IdP token-signing cert)", "technique": "T1606.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-03-08 22:08:08 UTC on ADFS-CORP-01.corp.local (the corp ADFS server), AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe scriptblock issuing 'Get-ChildItem Cert:\\LocalMachine\\My' AND filtering on Subject matching 'ADFS' OR 'Token-Sign' OR 'SAML' with HasPrivateKey. Rule 92201 informational fire.", "chain_reason": "LocalMachine\\My on an ADFS server holds the IdP token-signing certificate — the private key that signs every legitimate SAML assertion issued to corp SPs. Enumeration is the precondition step of T1606.002 Golden SAML."},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Self-signed cert generation as forged IdP signer", "technique": "T1606.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-03-08 22:08:24, AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe call 'New-SelfSignedCertificate -Subject \"CN=Forged-IdP-Signer-220808\" -KeyAlgorithm RSA -KeyLength 2048 -NotAfter (Get-Date).AddDays(7) -CertStoreLocation Cert:\\CurrentUser\\My'. The literal 'Forged-IdP-Signer' in the subject is operator-named.", "chain_reason": "The attacker generates a self-signed RSA cert as their forged IdP signing key surrogate. The literal 'Forged-IdP-Signer' subject is unambiguous attacker intent — no legitimate corp PKI uses this naming."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Forged SAML AuthnResponse construction with Administrator / Domain Admins claims", "technique": "T1606.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-03-08 22:08:48, AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe scriptblock building a samlp:Response XML with: Issuer='https://idp.corp.local/adfs/services/trust', Destination='https://sp.corp.local/SAML/ACS', NameID='Administrator', AttributeStatement groups='Domain Admins'. Sysmon EID 11 confirmed creation of forged-saml-signed.xml in user Temp. The SAML was signed with the forged cert via System.Security.Cryptography.Xml.SignedXml.", "chain_reason": "The SAML assertion claims Administrator identity AND Domain Admins group membership AND will be valid for 8 hours (NotOnOrAfter). With the forged signature, the corp SP (https://sp.corp.local/SAML/ACS) will accept and grant Administrator+Domain Admin access — EVEN THOUGH the legitimate ADFS never issued this token and no MFA was performed."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "ADFS audit log: NO matching token-issuance event + AMSI tool literals + POST to SP", "technique": "T1606.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-03-08 22:09:18, Sysmon EID 3 captured outbound HTTPS POST from powershell.exe to https://sp.corp.local/SAML/ACS with body SAMLResponse=<base64> and UA 'Mozilla/5.0 (forged SAML test)'. CORRELATION: ADFS-CORP-01 audit log (Microsoft-Windows-AD FS/Admin event 1200) shows NO matching SAML token-issuance event for Administrator destined to sp.corp.local in the 22:09:00-22:09:30 window. AMSI EID 4104 captured tool literals: 'mimikatz.exe \"ts::saml /idp:idp.corp.local /user:Administrator\"', 'shimit.py -idp ADFS -pk idp.pfx -u Administrator -g \"Domain Admins\"', 'AADInternals Open-AADIntOffice365Portal -SAMLToken <forged>', 'Solorigate-style: extract ADFS DKM master key + forge tokens'. Rule 60190 + 4104 triggered.", "chain_reason": "The forensic asymmetry — SP receiving a SAML assertion at a moment when ADFS did NOT issue any — is the definitive Golden SAML detection signal. Plus the literal AMSI references to shimit / AADInternals / Solorigate confirm operator awareness of the technique. Plus 'Mozilla/5.0 (forged SAML test)' UA is operator-named."}
  ],
